ML_PAGE("l4-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l4\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 4 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain, apply to the scenario, and justify. The model answers below show the standard to aim for; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l4\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>Raw information → numerical representation</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. What's hard?</b><p>Why the raw form (sentence, timestamp, image) can't be used directly.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Representation</b><p>What the technique turns it into (count vector, date parts, descriptor).</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. What it keeps / loses</b><p>Order, meaning, spatial relationships, time zone…</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. When to choose it</b><p>Compare with the nearest alternative and tie it to the scenario.</p></div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p>\"TF-IDF is better than Bag of Words.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">A verdict with no mechanism and no condition.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Bag of Words represents each document by raw word counts, so words that appear in every document, such as 'machine' in all maintenance notes, dominate. TF-IDF multiplies term frequency by inverse document frequency, log(N/df), so common words get weights near zero and distinctive terms such as 'bearing_failure' are emphasised. Both still ignore word order beyond any n-grams used.\"</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n<h3 class=\"block-title\">Model-answer bank</h3>\n<div class=\"scenario-grid\">\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>Explain the purpose of n-grams.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-1\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-1\">Bag of Words treats words independently and discards order. N-grams are contiguous sequences of n words — bigrams such as \"machine learning\", trigrams such as \"machine learning model\" — used as features to keep local word order. This captures phrases and negation: \"not good\" becomes one feature instead of \"not\" and \"good\". The cost is a much larger vocabulary.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>How does topic modelling differ from document clustering?</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-2\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-2\">Both are unsupervised. Clustering asks which documents belong together: documents become TF-IDF vectors and are grouped by similarity, each document going to one cluster. Topic modelling (LDA) asks what hidden topics exist: each topic is a distribution over terms (dog, cat, pet → Animals), and each document is represented as a mixture of topics. Humans interpret and name the topics.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare CBOW and skip-gram, and explain what embeddings represent.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-3\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-3\">Word embeddings are learned dense vectors in which semantically similar words lie close together, unlike one-hot vectors where every word is equally distant. Word2Vec learns them by prediction: CBOW predicts a target word from its surrounding context, while skip-gram predicts the context words from a target word. The vectors emerge as the network's internal representation.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">EXPLAIN</span>\n        <h4>How can timestamps become useful features, and why normalise time zones?</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-4\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-4\">A raw timestamp doesn't expose patterns such as \"weekday afternoon\", so extract components — year, month, day, hour, day-of-week, quarter, weekend — bin hours into time-of-day categories, and compute elapsed time via epoch or ordinal values. Time zones must first be normalised to UTC, because 14:00 in Malaysia and 06:00 in London can be the same instant; without UTC the model sees false eight-hour gaps.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">COMPARE</span>\n        <h4>Compare grayscale/histograms, edge/HOG features and CNNs.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-5\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-5\">An image is an (m, n, 3) matrix. Grayscale reduces it to (m, n); histograms and statistics summarise intensity but lose spatial information. Canny edges and HOG capture shape through intensity changes and edge orientations, while SURF describes local keypoints. These are hand-crafted. CNNs learn features automatically, from edges in early layers to complex concepts in deeper layers, at the cost of needing more data and computation.</div>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"scenario-card\"><span class=\"scenario-label\">SCENARIO</span>\n        <h4>Mixed data: machine_type, temperature, maintenance_comment, timestamp, inspection image.</h4>\n        <button data-reveal=\"s-l4-6\">Model answer</button>\n        <div class=\"scenario-answer\" id=\"s-l4-6\">machine_type is nominal → one-hot. temperature is numeric → round away sensor noise or bin into Normal/Warm/Critical. maintenance_comment is text → clean, then TF-IDF. timestamp → convert to UTC and extract hour, day of week, month and a night-shift flag. The image → intensity, edge or HOG features, or a CNN. All transformers are fitted on the training split and reused on the test split to avoid leakage.</div>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Practice questions and worked answers from the notes</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P1</span><span>Likely temporal subjective question</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain why extracting components from timestamps can improve an ML model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A raw timestamp contains several potentially meaningful temporal components such as year, month, day, weekday and hour. Extracting these creates structured numerical or categorical features that allow the model to learn patterns associated with seasonality, days of the week or times of day. Additional transformations can create higher-level features such as Morning or Evening. When timestamps come from different regions, timezone normalization is also important so that equivalent moments are represented consistently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P2</span><span>Mixed-data scenario</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A factory wants to predict equipment failure using Temperature, Machine Type, Maintenance Comment, Timestamp and Inspection Image.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should reason variable by variable.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Possible engineering</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature</td>\n<td>Numeric</td>\n<td>transform/bin if useful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine Type</td>\n<td>Categorical</td>\n<td>nominal encoding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance Comment</td>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>BoW / TF-IDF / embedding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timestamp</td>\n<td>Temporal</td>\n<td>hour/day/month/time-of-day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inspection Image</td>\n<td>Image</td>\n<td>pixels/descriptors/CNN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then explain:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These transformations convert each raw data type into a numerical representation appropriate for the model while retaining information relevant to the failure-prediction task.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is exactly the kind of integrative subjective answer I would expect.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">P3</span><span>Likely Lecture 4 subjective questions</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>The highest-value questions to prepare are:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare Bag of Words and TF-IDF.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of n-grams.</p>\n<p>Explain cosine similarity for document comparison.</p>\n<p>Explain how topic modelling differs from document clustering.</p>\n<p>Explain what word embeddings represent.</p>\n<p>Compare CBOW and Skip-gram.</p>\n<p>Explain how timestamps can be converted into useful features.</p>\n<p>Explain why timezone normalization matters.</p>\n<p>Explain how images become numerical data.</p>\n<p>Compare grayscale, histograms, edge/HOG features and CNN-based feature extraction.</p>\n<p>Explain Visual Bag of Words.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l4\" id=\"l4s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">41 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"LECTURE 4 — TEXT, TEMPORAL AND IMAGE FEATURE ENGINEERING\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s0\" title=\"LECTURE 4 — TEXT, TEMPORAL AND IMAGE FEATURE ENGINEERING\">Overview</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 1 — Why text is difficult\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s1\" title=\"Part 1 — Why text is difficult\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Text to numbers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 — Text preprocessing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s2\" title=\"Part 2 — Text preprocessing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Text preprocessing</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 — Tokenization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s3\" title=\"Part 3 — Tokenization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Tokenization</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 4 — Stopword removal\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s4\" title=\"Part 4 — Stopword removal\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Stopwords</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 — Stemming and lemmatization\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s5\" title=\"Part 5 — Stemming and lemmatization\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Stemming vs lemmatization</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 — Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s6\" title=\"Part 6 — Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 7 — Why is it called a “bag”?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s7\" title=\"Part 7 — Why is it called a “bag”?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>BoW ignores word order</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 — N-grams\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s8\" title=\"Part 8 — N-grams\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>N-grams</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 — Why are n-grams useful?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s9\" title=\"Part 9 — Why are n-grams useful?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Why n-grams help</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 10 — TF-IDF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s10\" title=\"Part 10 — TF-IDF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>TF-IDF</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 — TF-IDF formula intuition\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s11\" title=\"Part 11 — TF-IDF formula intuition\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>TF-IDF formula</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 — BoW vs TF-IDF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s12\" title=\"Part 12 — BoW vs TF-IDF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>BoW vs TF-IDF</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 — Document similarity\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s13\" title=\"Part 13 — Document similarity\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Cosine similarity</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 — Document clustering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s14\" title=\"Part 14 — Document clustering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Document clustering</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 — Topic modelling with LDA\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s15\" title=\"Part 15 — Topic modelling with LDA\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Topic modeling (LDA)</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 — Word embeddings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s16\" title=\"Part 16 — Word embeddings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Word embeddings</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 17 — Word2Vec: CBOW and Skip-gram\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s17\" title=\"Part 17 — Word2Vec: CBOW and Skip-gram\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>CBOW vs skip-gram</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 18 — Classic text features vs embeddings\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s18\" title=\"Part 18 — Classic text features vs embeddings\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Counts vs embeddings</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 19 — Temporal data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s19\" title=\"Part 19 — Temporal data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Temporal data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 20 — Date feature extraction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s20\" title=\"Part 20 — Date feature extraction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Date parts</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 21 — Time-of-day features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s21\" title=\"Part 21 — Time-of-day features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Time-of-day bins</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 22 — Time zones and UTC\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s22\" title=\"Part 22 — Time zones and UTC\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Time zones and UTC</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 23 — Epoch and Gregorian ordinal\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s23\" title=\"Part 23 — Epoch and Gregorian ordinal\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Epoch and ordinal dates</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 24 — Likely temporal subjective question\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s24\" title=\"Part 24 — Likely temporal subjective question\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>Model answer: timestamps</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 25 — Image data\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s25\" title=\"Part 25 — Image data\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>Images as numbers</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 26 — RGB images\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s26\" title=\"Part 26 — RGB images\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>RGB channels</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 27 — Grayscale\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s27\" title=\"Part 27 — Grayscale\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>Grayscale</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 28 — Flattening images\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s28\" title=\"Part 28 — Flattening images\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Flattening pixels</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 29 — Image intensity histograms\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s29\" title=\"Part 29 — Image intensity histograms\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Intensity histograms</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 30 — Statistical image features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s30\" title=\"Part 30 — Statistical image features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">30</span>Image statistics</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 31 — Edge detection\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s31\" title=\"Part 31 — Edge detection\"><span class=\"toc-no\">31</span>Edge detection</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 32 — HOG\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s32\" title=\"Part 32 — HOG\"><span class=\"toc-no\">32</span>HOG</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 33 — SURF\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s33\" title=\"Part 33 — SURF\"><span class=\"toc-no\">33</span>SURF</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 34 — Visual Bag of Words\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s34\" title=\"Part 34 — Visual Bag of Words\"><span class=\"toc-no\">34</span>Visual Bag of Words</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 35 — CNNs and automated feature extraction\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s35\" title=\"Part 35 — CNNs and automated feature extraction\"><span class=\"toc-no\">35</span>CNNs</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 36 — Traditional image features vs CNN\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s36\" title=\"Part 36 — Traditional image features vs CNN\"><span class=\"toc-no\">36</span>Hand-crafted vs CNN</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 37 — End-to-end integration\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s37\" title=\"Part 37 — End-to-end integration\"><span class=\"toc-no\">37</span>Pipeline order</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 38 — Mixed-data scenario\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s38\" title=\"Part 38 — Mixed-data scenario\"><span class=\"toc-no\">38</span>Scenario: mixed data</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 39 — Likely Lecture 4 subjective questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s39\" title=\"Part 39 — Likely Lecture 4 subjective questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">39</span>Likely questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 40 — The deepest distinction across Lectures 3 and 4\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l4s-s40\" title=\"Part 40 — The deepest distinction across Lectures 3 and 4\"><span class=\"toc-no\">40</span>Lecture 3 vs Lecture 4</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l4\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Lecture 4 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>41 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">LECTURE 4 — TEXT, TEMPORAL AND IMAGE FEATURE ENGINEERING</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecture 4 asks a deeper version of the same question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How can a model learn from information that isn't naturally represented as a simple numerical column?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The three major sources are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Text}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Temporal Data}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Images}$$</div>\n<p>The common goal is still:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Raw Information}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Numerical Feature Representation}\n}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 — Why text is difficult</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture describes text as unstructured and says that sentences must be converted into mathematical vectors before they can be used effectively by ML models.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;The machine has abnormal vibration.&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The algorithm needs some numerical representation of that sentence.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 — Text preprocessing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_text_pipeline\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Text preprocessing pipeline</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type any sentence and watch each preprocessing step.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Before feature extraction, the lecture introduces:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">tokenization\nlowercasing\nspecial-character removal\ncontraction expansion\nstopword removal\nspelling correction\nstemming\nlemmatization\n</code></pre>\n<p>The purpose is to make the text more consistent before building features.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 — Tokenization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Tokenization splits text into smaller units.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;machine failure detected&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">[&quot;machine&quot;, &quot;failure&quot;, &quot;detected&quot;]\n</code></pre>\n<p>This allows later feature-extraction methods to treat individual terms or sequences as features.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 — Stopword removal</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Common words such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">the\nis\nat\n</code></pre>\n<p>often occur frequently but add little useful distinction.</p>\n<p>Removing them can reduce noise.</p>\n<p>A good subjective explanation:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Stopword removal reduces high-frequency low-information terms so that more meaningful words contribute more strongly to the feature representation.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 — Stemming and lemmatization</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Both attempt to normalize related word forms.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">run\nruns\nrunning\n</code></pre>\n<p>may be reduced toward:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">run\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture treats stemming and lemmatization as normalization methods before feature extraction.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 — Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Bag of Words converts a document into a vector based on <strong>word frequencies</strong>.</p>\n<p>Suppose the vocabulary is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">cat\ndog\nrain\nsky\n</code></pre>\n<p>Document:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;cat dog dog&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,2,0,0]$$</div>\n<p>The lecture describes BoW as producing a numeric feature vector whose dimension corresponds to the distinct terms in the corpus.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 — Why is it called a “bag”?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Because simple Bag of Words ignores:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">word order\nsyntax\nsemantics\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly states this limitation.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog bites man\n</code></pre>\n<p>and:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">man bites dog\n</code></pre>\n<p>contain the same basic word counts.</p>\n<p>Their meanings are rather less interchangeable.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 — N-grams</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>N-grams preserve some local sequence information.</p>\n<p>Unigram:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine\nlearning\nmodel\n</code></pre>\n<p>Bigram:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning\nlearning model\n</code></pre>\n<p>Trigram:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">machine learning model\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture defines n-grams as contiguous word sequences.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 — Why are n-grams useful?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Consider:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">good\n</code></pre>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not good\n</code></pre>\n<p>Simple BoW treats:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">not\ngood\n</code></pre>\n<p>as separate terms.</p>\n<p>A bigram can represent:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;not good&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>as a feature.</p>\n<p>The lecture specifically notes this advantage for capturing negation and local word order.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 — TF-IDF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Bag of Words has another problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Very common words can dominate merely because they occur frequently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>TF-IDF tries to reduce this problem.</p>\n<p>It combines:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TF} = \\text{Term Frequency}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF} = \\text{Inverse Document Frequency}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture explains its central principle as:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Words appearing everywhere are penalized, while terms that distinguish particular documents receive greater weight.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 — TF-IDF formula intuition</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TFIDF} = \\text{TF} \\times \\text{IDF}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{IDF}(t)=\\log\\left(\\frac{N}{\\text{df}(t)}\\right)$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$N = \\text{total number of documents}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{df}(t)=\\text{number of documents containing term}\\ t$$</div>\n<p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;the&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>appears in every document.</p>\n<p>It has low discriminatory value.</p>\n<p>But:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">&quot;bearing_failure&quot;\n</code></pre>\n<p>appears in only a few maintenance reports.</p>\n<p>That term may be highly informative.</p>\n<p>So:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Common Everywhere}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{Lower Weight}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Rare but Relevant}\n\\Rightarrow\n\\text{Higher Weight}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 — BoW vs TF-IDF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_tfidf\"><div class=\"vis-title\">BoW, TF-IDF and cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Edit the documents; Bag-of-Words counts, TF-IDF weights and cosine similarities update live.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is an excellent subjective comparison.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Bag of Words</th>\n<th>TF-IDF</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Uses raw word frequencies</td>\n<td>Uses weighted frequencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequent words can dominate</td>\n<td>Common terms are penalized</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple</td>\n<td>More discriminative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ignores semantics</td>\n<td>Still largely frequency-based</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A strong concluding sentence:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>TF-IDF improves on raw Bag-of-Words counts by considering not only how frequently a word occurs in one document but also how common that word is across the entire corpus.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s13\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 — Document similarity</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_cosine\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Cosine similarity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Cosine similarity measures the angle between document vectors, not their length.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Once documents are vectors, they can be mathematically compared.</p>\n<p>The lecture uses <strong>cosine similarity</strong>.</p>\n<p>Conceptually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Similarity}=\n\\cos(\\theta)$$</div>\n<p>If two vectors point in similar directions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\theta\\approx0^\\circ$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(0^\\circ)=1$$</div>\n<p>High similarity.</p>\n<p>If:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\theta\\approx90^\\circ$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\cos(90^\\circ)=0$$</div>\n<p>Low similarity.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 — Document clustering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Now connect Lecture 4 back to Lecture 1.</p>\n<p>Documents:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{TFIDF vectors}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{similarity}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow \\text{clustering}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture shows documents about animals grouping separately from documents about weather and describes this as an unsupervised clustering problem.</p>\n<p>So feature engineering makes the text usable for the later ML task.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 — Topic modelling with LDA</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Clustering groups similar documents.</p>\n<p>Topic modelling asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What hidden themes exist across the collection?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture describes LDA as representing each document as a mixture of several topics, with each topic represented through collections of terms.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dog\ncat\nanimal\npet\n</code></pre>\n<p>may indicate one topic.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">rain\ncloud\nstorm\nweather\n</code></pre>\n<p>may indicate another.</p>\n<p>The topic itself is latent; humans interpret it based on its important words.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 — Word embeddings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_embed3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Word embeddings in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · A toy word-embedding space: similar words cluster, and directions encode relationships.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Bag of Words and TF-IDF mainly represent <strong>occurrence</strong>.</p>\n<p>Word embeddings try to represent <strong>semantic relationships</strong>.</p>\n<p>The lecture defines embeddings as numerical vectors where semantically similar words tend to appear closer to each other.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">king\nqueen\n</code></pre>\n<p>should be closer than:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">king\ncar\n</code></pre>\n<p>because their linguistic contexts are more related.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 — Word2Vec: CBOW and Skip-gram</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture introduces two basic Word2Vec ideas.</p>\n<h5>CBOW</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Context}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Predict Target Word}$$</div>\n<h5>Skip-gram</h5>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Target Word}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Predict Context}$$</div>\n<p>The key subjective idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>By learning these prediction tasks, the model develops useful numerical word representations.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 18 — Classic text features vs embeddings</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>You can compare them like this:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Classical text features</th>\n<th>Embeddings</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Count/frequency based</td>\n<td>Learned numerical representations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Often sparse</td>\n<td>Dense vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>BoW/TF-IDF</td>\n<td>Word2Vec-style embeddings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Limited semantic understanding</td>\n<td>Better semantic relationships</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Don't oversell embeddings beyond what the lecture says. The source's main point is that semantically similar words receive nearby representations.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 19 — Temporal data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Temporal data contains:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">dates\ntimes\ntimestamps\ntime zones\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture emphasizes that useful features often need to be extracted from these raw values before modelling.</p>\n<p>A timestamp such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">2026-10-05 14:32:15\n</code></pre>\n<p>contains several possible features.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 20 — Date feature extraction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_timestamp\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Timestamp feature extraction</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · One timestamp becomes many features — and the same instant looks different in each time zone.</figcaption></figure></div><p>From one timestamp we can derive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">year\nmonth\nday\nquarter\nday of week\nday name\nweek of year\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture explicitly lists these examples.</p>\n<p>Why does this help?</p>\n<p>Because:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Monday\nDecember\nQ4\nWeekend\n</code></pre>\n<p>may have predictive meaning that the raw timestamp does not expose directly.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 21 — Time-of-day features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>We can similarly extract:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">hour\nminute\n</code></pre>\n<p>and then engineer:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Morning\nAfternoon\nEvening\nNight\n</code></pre>\n<p>using binning.</p>\n<p>The lecture shows this exact workflow, including hour-based time-of-day ranges.</p>\n<p>This demonstrates something important:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Feature-engineering techniques combine.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Temporal extraction gives:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Hour}$$</div>\n<p>then numeric binning converts it to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TimeOfDay}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 22 — Time zones and UTC</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia: 14:00 UTC+8\nLondon:    06:00 UTC+0\n</code></pre>\n<p>These may refer to the <strong>same moment</strong>.</p>\n<p>If you ignore the timezone:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>the model may think the events occurred eight hours apart.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The lecture therefore introduces UTC normalization for global temporal data.</p>\n<p>Strong exam sentence:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Timezone normalization prevents equivalent moments recorded in different local time zones from being incorrectly treated as different points in time.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 23 — Epoch and Gregorian ordinal</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture also introduces alternate numerical representations.</p>\n<h5>Epoch</h5>\n<p>Number of seconds since:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$1\\ \\text{January}\\ 1970\\ \\text{UTC}$$</div>\n<h5>Gregorian ordinal</h5>\n<p>A continuous count of days beginning from an early calendar reference.</p>\n<p>Why useful?</p>\n<p>Because they make arithmetic easier.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{TimeElapsed}\n=\n\\text{CurrentEpoch}\n-\n\\text{PreviousEpoch}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture specifically mentions creating elapsed-time features from important events.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 24 — Likely temporal subjective question</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><blockquote>\n<p>Explain why extracting components from timestamps can improve an ML model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>A strong answer:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A raw timestamp contains several potentially meaningful temporal components such as year, month, day, weekday and hour. Extracting these creates structured numerical or categorical features that allow the model to learn patterns associated with seasonality, days of the week or times of day. Additional transformations can create higher-level features such as Morning or Evening. When timestamps come from different regions, timezone normalization is also important so that equivalent moments are represented consistently.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 25 — Image data</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture's starting principle is extremely simple:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Image} = \\text{Matrix of Numerical Pixel Values}\n}$$</div>\n<p>A computer does not begin with:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>“That is a Labrador.”</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It begins with numbers.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 26 — RGB images</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_rgb3d\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Images as matrices in 3D</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>3D · An 8×8 image as RGB channel matrices, then grayscale, then a flattened vector.</figcaption></figure></div><p>A colour image has three channels:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R,\\ G,\\ B$$</div>\n<p>Its shape can be represented as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,c)$$</div>\n<p>where:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$m = \\text{rows}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$n = \\text{columns}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$c = \\text{channel}$$</div>\n<p>and typically:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$c=3$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 27 — Grayscale</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A colour image can be converted from:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n,3)$$</div>\n<p>to roughly:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$(m,n)$$</div>\n<p>The lecture gives grayscale intensity as:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$Y=\n0.2125R+\n0.7154G+\n0.0721B$$</div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>It reduces dimensionality while retaining intensity information.</p>\n<p>Useful if colour itself is not essential.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 28 — Flattening images</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>A two-dimensional pixel matrix can be flattened into a one-dimensional vector.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\begin{bmatrix}\n0.2 &amp; 0.5\\\\\n0.7 &amp; 0.9\n\\end{bmatrix}$$</div>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[0.2,0.5,0.7,0.9]$$</div>\n<p>The lecture notes that raw pixel matrices can therefore be used as ordinary ML input vectors.</p>\n<p>The drawback:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A simple flattened representation does not naturally preserve spatial relationships between neighbouring pixels.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 29 — Image intensity histograms</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Instead of using every pixel directly, you can summarize the distribution of intensities.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0.0–0.1 → 20 pixels\n0.1–0.2 → 50\n...\n0.8–0.9 → 200\n</code></pre>\n<p>The lecture uses the Labrador example to show how a lighter image produces more high-intensity values.</p>\n<p>So a histogram captures:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>how bright/dark the image is overall,</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>but loses detailed spatial location.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 30 — Statistical image features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture also extracts summary statistics such as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">mean\nmedian\nvariance\nskewness\nkurtosis\n</code></pre>\n<p>from pixel distributions.</p>\n<p>This reduces a huge pixel matrix to a smaller numerical feature set.</p>\n<p>Trade-off:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Compactness}$$</div>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Loss of Detail}$$</div>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 31 — Edge detection</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l4_edges\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Edges and HOG</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · From pixels to edges to HOG-style orientation features.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Edges occur where image intensity changes sharply.</p>\n<p>The lecture introduces the <strong>Canny edge detector</strong> as a widely used method for detecting those sharp changes.</p>\n<p>Why useful?</p>\n<p>Because edges can represent:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">boundaries\nshape\ncontours\nareas of interest\n</code></pre>\n<p>rather than raw pixel brightness alone.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 32 — HOG</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>HOG stands for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Histogram of Oriented Gradients}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture presents it as a technique used in object detection.</p>\n<p>Instead of only asking:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where are edges?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>HOG also captures:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>What directions are those gradients/edges pointing?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That helps describe:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">shape\ncontour\nsilhouette\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 33 — SURF</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>SURF stands for:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Speeded Up Robust Features}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture presents it as a localized feature-extraction method that identifies interesting points or regions and produces descriptors around them.</p>\n<p>Mental idea:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Instead of summarizing the whole image equally, identify distinctive local regions.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 34 — Visual Bag of Words</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This is conceptually clever because it borrows an idea from text.</p>\n<p>Text BoW:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Document → Words → Word Counts\"><span>Document</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Words</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Word Counts</span></div>\n<p>Visual BoW:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Image → Local Descriptors → Clusters → Visual Words → Visual Word Counts\"><span>Image</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Local Descriptors</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Clusters</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Visual Words</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Visual Word Counts</span></div>\n<p>The lecture describes clustering local feature descriptors into visual words and then representing the image as a vector of counts.</p>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$[1,3,5]$$</div>\n<p>might represent counts of several visual feature types.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 35 — CNNs and automated feature extraction</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Traditional image ML:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pixels → Human-designed features → Model\"><span>Pixels</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Human-designed features</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Model</span></div>\n<p>For example:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">HOG\nSURF\nhistograms\n</code></pre>\n<p>CNN approach:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Pixels → CNN → Automatically Learned Features → Prediction\"><span>Pixels</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>CNN</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Automatically Learned Features</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Prediction</span></div>\n<p>The lecture presents CNNs as a shift toward automated feature extraction.</p>\n<p>The general intuition is:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">early layers → simple visual patterns\ndeeper layers → more complex visual patterns\n</code></pre>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 36 — Traditional image features vs CNN</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_cnn_layers\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>A CNN replaces hand-designed extractors with layers that learn increasingly abstract features.</figcaption></figure></div><p>A strong comparison:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Traditional image-feature engineering requires manually designed representations such as pixel statistics, edge detection, HOG or localized descriptors. CNNs instead learn useful visual representations automatically from raw image data during training. Therefore, classical approaches depend more heavily on handcrafted features, while deep learning shifts part of the feature-engineering process into the model itself.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is exactly the conceptual transition the slides are illustrating.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 37 — End-to-end integration</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The lecture finishes by integrating everything.</p>\n<p>The correct workflow is essentially:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train/Test Split}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Fit Feature Transformers on Training Data}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Transform Training}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Transform Test Using Existing Transformers}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Train Model}\n\\rightarrow\n\\text{Evaluate}$$</div>\n<p>This connects Lecture 4 straight back to Lecture 2.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 38 — Mixed-data scenario</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"l4_mixed_hub\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Each data type gets its own representation — and every transformer is fitted on training data only.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A factory wants to predict equipment failure using Temperature, Machine Type, Maintenance Comment, Timestamp and Inspection Image.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You should reason variable by variable.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Possible engineering</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Temperature</td>\n<td>Numeric</td>\n<td>transform/bin if useful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine Type</td>\n<td>Categorical</td>\n<td>nominal encoding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maintenance Comment</td>\n<td>Text</td>\n<td>BoW / TF-IDF / embedding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timestamp</td>\n<td>Temporal</td>\n<td>hour/day/month/time-of-day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inspection Image</td>\n<td>Image</td>\n<td>pixels/descriptors/CNN</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Then explain:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>These transformations convert each raw data type into a numerical representation appropriate for the model while retaining information relevant to the failure-prediction task.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That is exactly the kind of integrative subjective answer I would expect.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 39 — Likely Lecture 4 subjective questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The highest-value questions to prepare are:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Compare Bag of Words and TF-IDF.</p>\n<p>Explain the purpose of n-grams.</p>\n<p>Explain cosine similarity for document comparison.</p>\n<p>Explain how topic modelling differs from document clustering.</p>\n<p>Explain what word embeddings represent.</p>\n<p>Compare CBOW and Skip-gram.</p>\n<p>Explain how timestamps can be converted into useful features.</p>\n<p>Explain why timezone normalization matters.</p>\n<p>Explain how images become numerical data.</p>\n<p>Compare grayscale, histograms, edge/HOG features and CNN-based feature extraction.</p>\n<p>Explain Visual Bag of Words.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l4s-s40\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 40 — The deepest distinction across Lectures 3 and 4</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecture 3 mostly asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do I improve already structured numeric and categorical variables?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Lecture 4 asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How do I convert more complex raw information into structured numerical representations?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So the whole chapter can be reduced to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Feature Engineering}\n=\n\\text{Choosing}\\ a\\ \\text{representation that exposes useful information to the model}\n}$$</div>\n<p>For numbers, that may mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Log},\\ \\text{Binning},\\ \\text{Interaction}$$</div>\n<p>For categories:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{OneHot},\\ \\text{Dummy},\\ \\text{Hashing}$$</div>\n<p>For text:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{BoW},\\ \\text{TFIDF},\\ \\text{Embeddings}$$</div>\n<p>For time:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Month},\\ \\text{Hour},\\ \\text{Weekday},\\ \\text{ElapsedTime}$$</div>\n<p>For images:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Pixels},\\ \\text{Histograms},\\ \\text{HOG},\\ \\text{SURF},\\ \\text{CNN}$$</div>\n<p>The lecture's final takeaway is essentially that raw data is not automatically a useful feature representation, good features can matter more than a sophisticated algorithm, and domain knowledge is critical in deciding what representation is meaningful.</p>\n<p>For the subjective paper, keep coming back to three questions:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{What problem does this transformation solve}?\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Why is it appropriate for this data type}?\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{What information is gained or lost}?\n}$$</div>\n<p>If you answer those three instead of merely naming techniques, your answers move from definition-level to actual analysis.</p></div></details></div></details>");
